   "gap": "Reconstruct the remaining selected estimation arguments of Fisher 1922 so that each estimate and theorem has explicit model, sampling and regularity scope: (i) the large-sample probable-error/efficiency argument for maximum likelihood (pp.328-332, including the multiparameter Hessian-determinant rule on p.332) as a stated theorem with exact existence, identification, localization, Taylor-remainder and limit conditions and a written proof, not only the finite common-support analogue thm:p15-fe-score; (ii) the sec.7 sufficiency argument (pp.330-331) in a correct general form (e.g. a factorization criterion and the conditions under which the maximizer is a function of a sufficient statistic), beyond the finite definition and the countermodel; (iii) sec.8 efficiency of the method of moments for Pearson Type III (location, scale, shape and joint estimation, from p.332); (iv) location and scaling of frequency curves in general / intrinsic accuracy, and the Pearson-curve regimes including Type VII and near-normal moment efficiency (e.g. p.353 table); (v) the Poisson-series and dilution-method discontinuous-distribution arguments; (vi) for grouped data: a uniform bound on the fixed-phase periodic remainder of the grouped-normal information, existence/uniqueness and a limit theorem for the grouped MLE, and a calibrated sampling distribution for the goodness-of-fit discrepancies D/Q. fisher-estimation-and-regularity.tex lines 13-18 and 231-242 and fisher-grouping-and-moments.tex lines 207-219 and 350-363 state these are not supplied.",
